==English==
[[File:04565_Christmas_dumplings_with_dried_plums.JPG|thumb|A plate of '''pierogi'''.]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|pierogie|pierogy|pirogi|pirogy|pirojki|pyrogy|pyrohy}}
* {{alter|en|perog|pierog|pieróg||from the Polish singular}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|pl|pierogi}}, the plural of {{m|pl|pieróg||dumpling}}, which ultimately is derived from {{der|en|sla-pro|*pirъ||party}}. Unrelated to {{noncog|tr|börek}}. {{doublet|en|pirogi}} (from Russian), {{m|en|pirohy}} (from Czech and Slovak), and {{m|en|pyrohy}}/{{m|en|pedaheh}} (from Ukrainian).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pəˈɹoːɡi/}}, {{IPA|en|/pɛˈɾoːɡi/}} (amongst Polish Americans)
* {{audio|en|en-ne-pierogi.ogg}}
* {{hyphenation|en|pier|ogi}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pierogi|pierogies|s}}

# {{lb|en|North America}} A [[square]]- or [[crescent]]-shaped [[dumpling]] of [[unleavened]] [[dough]], [[stuff]]ed with [[sauerkraut]], [[cheese]], [[mash]]ed [[potatoes]], [[cabbage]], [[onion]], [[meat]], or any combination of these, or with a [[fruit]] [[filling]].

====Usage notes====
* Russian "[[pirozhki]]" ({{l|ru||пирожки́}}) and "[[pirogi]]" (singular "[[pirog]]", {{cog|ru||пироги́}}) and Polish pierogi (diminutive: "pierożki") (dumplings) are often confused. The two dishes are quite different, as pirozhki are filled buns, baked or fried, and pirogi are pies; the Ukrainian version of the Polish pierogi are called varenyky. In some western regions of Ukraine, the term пироги́ may refer to the Ukrainian version the Polish "pierogi", while пиріжки́ are similar to the Russian "pirozhki".
* The use of ''pierogi'' instead of ''pierog'' or ''pieróg'' to refer to a single dumpling is proscribed by some.<ref>{{cite-book|en|author=Mitchell Davis|chapter=Pierogi|title=The Mensch Chef or Why Delicious Jewish Food Isn’t an Oxymoron|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Crown Publishing Group|Clarkson Potter/Publishers]]|year=2002|section=chapter 1 (Dinner Starts at Five: Appetizers and Salads)|page=18|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/menschcheforwhy00davi/page/18/mode/1up|isbn=0-609-80781-1|passage=Just in case you were wondering, ''pierogi'' is a Polish plural. ''Pierogis'' is grammatically incorrect. One is simply called a ''pierog''.}}</ref>

